course: dictionary
lesson: 9442
title: Stale: old and not fresh
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Stale means old and not fresh any more. It can describe food, air or even jokes. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: «The termites' mound lets stale air out and fresh air in.» Fresh air is clean and new, so stale air must be the old, stuffy air that needs to leave. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The same word can describe very different things. Bread that is left out goes stale and dry. A joke that people have heard too many times is stale and boring. Each meaning is about something being no longer new or fresh.
fact: Stale means old, dry and not fresh any more.
fact: Stale food, such as bread or cake, is hard and dry.
fact: Stale air is old and stuffy, like in a room with closed windows.
fact: Stale news or jokes are old and boring because people have heard them too many times.
fact: The opposite of stale is fresh.
exercise: «The bread was stale, so we toasted it.» What does stale mean here? | Sweet and sticky | *Old, dry and not fresh | Warm and soft | Full of seeds
exercise: «The room felt stale, so we opened the window.» What does stale air mean? | Bright and sunny | *Old and stuffy | Loud and noisy | Cool and clean
exercise: «Dad told the same stale joke again.» Why does the joke sound boring? | It was written in French | It made everyone laugh | It was too long | *People have heard it too many times
test: Which word is the opposite of stale? | sweet | hard | *fresh | old
test: «Bread goes stale as it loses water.» Which meaning fits stale best here? | Loud and crunchy | Bright and shiny | Full of holes | *Old, dry and not fresh
exam: Mia says stale air and a stale joke are both old and not fresh any more. What do they have in common? | *Both are no longer new or fresh | Both are loud | Both are sweet | Both are hot
